    {
      "id": "3cfb4b0d-0eb6-41af-a5f0-395a4eb32912",
      "name": "MCP turns AI from adviser into collaborator",
      "path": "WWW / MCP Field Notes / MCP turns AI from adviser into collaborator",
      "docTypeId": "7f1910d0-ae36-49f4-89c6-1282f5cf536f",
      "templateId": "04029e6d-a9f2-48e0-a5c3-a030465c3145",
      "state": "Published",
      "domains": [],
      "values": [
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.BlockGrid",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "blocks",
          "value": {
            "layout": {
              "Umbraco.BlockGrid": [
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "9b4d4b04-2465-44d4-b3ef-210a8c1b9a11",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "801f45f2-d684-4ee6-9611-ffef0db7e3be",
                  "settingsKey": null
                }
              ]
            },
            "contentData": [
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "cb57bafa-958a-431a-b296-d675ca38c09c",
                "key": "9b4d4b04-2465-44d4-b3ef-210a8c1b9a11",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "heading",
                    "value": "MCP turns AI from adviser into collaborator"
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextArea",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "summary",
                    "value": "The magic of MCP is not a smarter chat window. It is a shared, typed workspace where an AI can inspect reality, make precise changes, and prove what happened."
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.DateTime",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "publishedDate",
                    "value": "2026-10-03 00:00:00"
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.MediaPicker3",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "image",
                    "value": [
                      {
                        "key": "2795864a-ce7c-458b-9291-385230211552",
                        "mediaKey": "206b5c03-6c24-43e6-b76c-96876e2a794c",
                        "mediaTypeAlias": "Image",
                        "crops": [],
                        "focalPoint": null
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "imageAlt",
                    "value": "Two pairs of hands fitting puzzle pieces together"
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "imageCredit",
                    "value": "Photo by Vardan Papikyan on Unsplash"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "88cc0d03-dd9b-4da1-b87a-54bc6db24fcf",
                "key": "801f45f2-d684-4ee6-9611-ffef0db7e3be",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "body",
                    "value": {
                      "markup": "<p>Most AI conversations stop at advice: here is the API call, here is the JSON, here is what you should click. Model Context Protocol closes that last mile. It gives an assistant a discoverable set of tools and a consistent way to call them.</p><h2>Work happens where the truth lives</h2><p>In a CMS, the truth is the schema, the media library, and the content tree. Through MCP, an assistant can inspect those structures before acting. It can reuse an existing data type, put media in the right folder, publish a document, and read it back to verify the result.</p><p>That feedback loop is what makes MCP feel different. The assistant is no longer guessing from a screenshot or handing over a checklist. It can collaborate inside the same boundaries as the editor, while every operation remains explicit and reviewable.</p><h2>Capability without losing control</h2><p>MCP tools are narrow contracts rather than unlimited access. A server can expose exactly the operations a team is comfortable automating. The result is a useful balance: real agency, clear guardrails, and an audit trail made of concrete tool calls.</p>",
                      "blocks": {
                        "layout": {},
                        "contentData": [],
                        "settingsData": [],
                        "expose": []
                      }
                    }
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "settingsData": [],
            "expose": [
              {
                "contentKey": "9b4d4b04-2465-44d4-b3ef-210a8c1b9a11",
                "culture": null,
                "segment": null
              },
              {
                "contentKey": "801f45f2-d684-4ee6-9611-ffef0db7e3be",
                "culture": null,
                "segment": null
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
      "urls": [
        "/mcp-turns-ai-from-adviser-into-collaborator/"
      ],
      "docType": "blogPost",
      "template": "blogPost"
    },
    {
      "id": "8ffe7538-df2b-41b6-8881-cf25f68c417a",
      "name": "MCP makes integrations feel native",
      "path": "WWW / MCP Field Notes / MCP makes integrations feel native",
      "docTypeId": "7f1910d0-ae36-49f4-89c6-1282f5cf536f",
      "templateId": "04029e6d-a9f2-48e0-a5c3-a030465c3145",
      "state": "Published",
      "domains": [],
      "values": [
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.BlockGrid",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "blocks",
          "value": {
            "layout": {
              "Umbraco.BlockGrid": [
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "f533b063-088e-44a9-b0d4-d2c76fd90f03",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "3152d6f6-39cd-4fab-ada6-8e236f3c11bd",
                  "settingsKey": null
                }
              ]
            },
            "contentData": [
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "cb57bafa-958a-431a-b296-d675ca38c09c",
                "key": "f533b063-088e-44a9-b0d4-d2c76fd90f03",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "heading",
                    "value": "MCP makes integrations feel native"
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextArea",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "summary",
                    "value": "MCP replaces one-off glue with a discoverable contract. Connect a capable server once and every compatible client can understand how to work with it."
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.DateTime",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "publishedDate",
                    "value": "2026-10-02 00:00:00"
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.MediaPicker3",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "image",
                    "value": [
                      {
                        "key": "46a7d342-a4b3-4268-9481-4248e244af95",
                        "mediaKey": "314a4272-a74f-4765-8c71-f596e96ce78b",
                        "mediaTypeAlias": "Image",
                        "crops": [],
                        "focalPoint": null
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "imageAlt",
                    "value": "Four different USB cable connectors on a grey background"
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "imageCredit",
                    "value": "Photo by Hal Gatewood on Unsplash"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "88cc0d03-dd9b-4da1-b87a-54bc6db24fcf",
                "key": "3152d6f6-39cd-4fab-ada6-8e236f3c11bd",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "body",
                    "value": {
                      "markup": "<p>Integrations are usually a collection of private assumptions: an endpoint hidden in a wiki, a script only one person understands, or a plugin tied to one interface. Model Context Protocol turns those assumptions into a shared contract that clients can discover.</p><h2>One connection, many useful tools</h2><p>An MCP server can describe the actions it supports and the shapes those actions accept. A client does not need bespoke UI code for every capability. It can learn that Umbraco offers document types, templates, content, domains, and media operations, then choose the precise tool needed for the job.</p><p>That makes integrations feel native. The same assistant can move from a repository to a CMS without pretending they are the same system, because each server provides the vocabulary and guardrails for its own domain.</p><h2>Portable by design</h2><p>The protocol also separates capability from a particular model or chat product. Teams can invest in a well-designed server and reuse it across compatible clients. Better still, local servers keep development workflows close to the project, so an experiment can be powerful without sending control of the system elsewhere.</p><p>MCP is awesome because it makes interoperability practical: explicit tools, discoverable context, and fewer brittle bridges between people, models, and software.</p>",
                      "blocks": {
                        "layout": {},
                        "contentData": [],
                        "settingsData": [],
                        "expose": []
                      }
                    }
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "settingsData": [],
            "expose": [
              {
                "contentKey": "f533b063-088e-44a9-b0d4-d2c76fd90f03",
                "culture": null,
                "segment": null
              },
              {
                "contentKey": "3152d6f6-39cd-4fab-ada6-8e236f3c11bd",
                "culture": null,
                "segment": null
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
      "urls": [
        "/mcp-makes-integrations-feel-native/"
      ],
      "docType": "blogPost",
      "template": "blogPost"
    }
